The atlas plans Hasse invariants only in other settings:
- modular curves: AlgebraicModularFormsAndSerreWeights:R15.3, 'Construct the Hasse invariant, Frobenius/Verschiebung and theta operator';
- Hilbert varieties: HilbertModularVarietiesAndShimuraCurves:H2, 'Construct its Hasse ideal …', and ShimuraCompactifications:C6;
- Ekedahl–Oort Hasse sections in Caraiani–Scholze's Igusa geometry: IgusaVarietiesAndTorsionConcentration:IG.2.
No layer constructs h ∈ H^0(X_k, ω^{p−1}) for the genus-two Siegel threefold on its toroidal compactification, or an O-lift A of h^r. AbelianSchemesAndArithmeticModuli (A1–A6) has no Hasse invariant of an abelian scheme. Definition 6.1 is on p. 835. The paper also uses h and A in §8.4–8.6 (pp. 861–867), where it relies on these further facts. h has q-expansion 1 at every cusp. Its reduced zero locus is the non-ordinary locus (p-rank ≤ 1), so a form F ∈ H⁰(X, ω(a,b)_{O/ϖ}) vanishes there if and only if F = hG with G of weight (a−p+1, b−p+1). A has q-expansion ≡ 1 mod ϖ, and A^s with p^{m−1} | s has q-expansion ≡ 1 mod ϖ^m.

Ampleness is planned. ShimuraCompactifications:C5: 'Establish the Hodge-line positivity and graded-section finite-generation statements that yield the integral minimal compactification, then its projectivity'. PELModuli:M5 plans Siegel moduli ('Construct Siegel moduli for every dimension'). The rest is not planned:
- the Hasse invariant h itself (hasse-invariant-h-and-lift-A);
- the integral Koecher principle for the genus-two Siegel toroidal compactification (C6 plans Koecher only for Hilbert sections: 'For g>1, prove the precise Koecher extension theorem needed for Hilbert sections');
- the resulting lift of h^r. In fact every sufficiently large r works. ω is ample on the minimal compactification, so Serre vanishing kills H¹ of its high powers there, and reduction mod ϖ is then surjective on H⁰ in those weights. External input: Koecher principle (as recalled in §5.2; Lan [51]) together with the ampleness of ω on the minimal compactification of the Siegel moduli space (Faltings–Chai, Degeneration of abelian varieties, Ch. V; Lan)

items.statement:
Let μ = (a,b) ∈ X*(T)^+_M with a ≥ b ≥ 2. Multiplication by h defines an injection h: H^0(X_1(Q), ω(a,b)_k) ↪ H^0(X_1(Q), ω(a+(p−1), b+(p−1))_k). It maps the cuspidal subspace H^0(X_1(Q), ω(a,b)(−∞)_k) into H^0(X_1(Q), ω(a+p−1, b+p−1)(−∞)_k), so it is also injective there. It is equivariant for:
- the Hecke operators T_{x,i} for each x ∉ S ∪ Q ∪ {p};
- the operators U_{x,i} for x ∈ Q;
- the diamond operators ⟨u⟩, u ∈ Δ_Q. The paper does not state this case. Remark 6.5 needs it, and it holds because h is invariant under the automorphisms of X_1(Q) over X_0(Q).

Let μ = (a,b) ∈ X*(T)^+_M with a ≥ b ≥ 3; let w = a+b−6 and 𝐰 = w+3 = a+b−3. Let f ∈ H^0(X_1(Q), ω(a,b)(−∞)) (over O or K) be a cuspidal eigenform for the operators T_{x,i} for all x ∉ Q ∪ S and i = 0,1,2. Then there is a continuous semisimple representation r_f: G_Q → GSp4(K′), for a finite extension K′/K, such that:
(1) ν∘r_f = λ_f∘χ_μ·ε^{−3} = λ_f∘χ_{μ,0}·ε^{−𝐰};
(2) r_f is unramified at every prime x ∉ Q ∪ S ∪ {p}, and det(X − r_f(Frob_x)) = λ_f(Q_x(X)).

Let π be a cuspidal automorphic representation of GSp4(A_Q) whose archimedean component is a holomorphic discrete series (the regular weights of Proposition 6.8). Then there is a continuous semisimple R_p = R_{p,ι}(π): G_Q → GL4(Q̄_p) such that:
- R_p is unramified at primes x ∤ p where π_x is unramified, with Frobenius characteristic polynomial the (normalised) spinor Hecke polynomial;
- R_p is crystalline at p when π_p is unramified, with Hodge–Tate weights and crystalline Frobenius polynomial as in Proposition 6.8(3);
- R_p has the ordinary upper-triangular shape when π is p-ordinary;
- R_p satisfies local–global compatibility at all primes when it is absolutely irreducible.

With r̄ and q as above, set S := S(r̄) ∪ {q}, where S(r̄) is the set of primes x ≠ p at which r̄ is ramified. Let Q be a possibly empty finite set of primes disjoint from S ∪ {p}. Define K = ∏_x K_x ⊂ GSp4(A^∞) by:
(1) K_x = GSp4(Z_x) if x = p, or if r̄ is unramified at x and x ≠ q;
(2) K_x = I(x), the Iwahori subgroup, if x is of type U3;
(3) K_x = Π(x), the Klingen parahoric, if x is of type U2;
(4) K_x = K(x), the paramodular group at x, if x is of type U1;
(5) K_x = Π(x)^+ if x is of type P (and x − 1 is prime to p);
(6) K_x = the full congruence subgroup of level x if x is of type H;
(7) K_x = the full congruence subgroup of level x if x = q.
Then X = X_K and X_i(Q) = X_{K_i(Q)} as in §5.2, where K_i(Q) replaces K_x (x ∈ Q) by Π(x) (i = 0) or Π(x)^+ (i = 1).

In the setting of Theorem 6.13, let ψ be the unique square root of χ_{μ,0} that is trivial modulo m (it exists since χ_{μ,0} ≡ 1 mod m and p is odd). Then r ⊗ ψ^{−1} is a deformation of r̄ satisfying properties (2)–(5) of Definition 4.6:
(2) unramified at x ∉ Q ∪ S(r̄) ∪ {p};
(3) at x ∈ S(r̄) of type U1/U2/U3, r|I_x is unipotent with image topologically generated by exp(N), N nilpotent of rank 1/2/3;
(4) at x of type P, r(I_x) ≅ r̄(I_x);
(5) at x ∈ Q, r|G_x ≅ V_1 ⊕ V_2 with isotropic planes V_1 lifting λ(α_x) ⊕ λ(β_x) and V_2 lifting λ(γ_x) ⊕ λ(δ_x), with I_x acting on V_1 by scalars via a character and on V_2 by its inverse.

Let r̄: G_Q → GSp4(k), S = S(r̄) ∪ {q}, Q and K be as in §6.3, and let α, β ∈ k^× be as in §4. Let σ = (a,2) ∈ X*(T)^+_M with a ≥ 2 (a nonregular, limit of discrete series, weight). r̄ is Katz modular of weight σ if there is a maximal ideal m_∅ of T_σ such that:
(1) r̄_{m_∅} ≅ r̄;
(2) there is a form η ∈ H^0(X, ω(a,2)_{K/O})[m_∅] with T_{p,1}(η) = (α + β)η and Q_{p,2}(η) = (αβ)η.

Hypotheses:
- r̄: G_Q → GSp4(k) is as in §4 and §6.3, with auxiliary prime q, S = S(r̄) ∪ {q} and level K. In particular ν(r̄) = ε̄^{−(a−1)}, where a ≥ 2 is the integer of §4.
- Q is a finite set of primes disjoint from S ∪ {p}.
- σ = (a,2), with the same a as in §4, and 2 ≤ a < p − 1.
- m is a maximal ideal of T_σ(Q) containing m_∅.
- Assumption 6.16 holds, and (α²−1)(β²−1)(α−β)(α²β²−1) ≠ 0.
Then there is a continuous representation r_Q: G_Q → GSp4(T_σ(Q)^{α,β}_m) which is a minimal deformation of r̄ outside Q in the sense of Definition 4.6:
(1) ν(r_Q) = ε^{−(a−1)};
(2) r_Q is unramified outside Q ∪ S(r̄) ∪ {p};
(3) at x ∈ S(r̄) of type U1/U2/U3, r_Q|I_x is unipotent with image topologically generated by exp(N), N nilpotent of rank 1/2/3;
(4) r_Q(I_x) ≅ r̄(I_x) at primes of type P;
(5) at x ∈ Q, r_Q|G_x ≅ V_1 ⊕ V_2, with isotropic planes V_1 lifting λ(α_x) ⊕ λ(β_x) and V_2 lifting λ(γ_x) ⊕ λ(δ_x), and I_x acting on V_1 by a character and on V_2 by its inverse;
(6) r_Q|G_p ~ [[χ_αψ^{−1}, 0, ∗, ∗], [0, χ_βψ^{−1}, ∗, ∗], [0, 0, ε^{−(a−1)}χ_β^{−1}ψ, 0], [0, 0, 0, ε^{−(a−1)}χ_α^{−1}ψ]]. Here χ_α and χ_β are unramified characters lifting λ(α) and λ(β), and ψ is an unramified character trivial modulo m.

There is a form h̃ ∈ H^0(S, ω(p²−1, p²−1)_k) on the nonordinary locus S of X_k, and likewise on S_0(Q), with these properties:
- h̃ extends over the boundary of the toroidal compactification;
- multiplication by h̃ is Hecke-equivariant away from p;
- (stated but not used) h̃ vanishes to precise order 2 on the 1-dimensional Ekedahl–Oort stratum of S.

For the boundary ∂X of a toroidal compactification of the Siegel 3-fold, there is a nerve spectral sequence E_1^{r,s} = ⊕_{r(R)=r+1} E_1^{r,s}(R) ⇒ H^{r+s}(∂X, ω(a,2)_C). R runs over the Q-parabolics of G = GSp4 and r(R) is the parabolic rank. E_1(R)^{r,s} is the space of K-invariants (K the level) in
Ind_{R(A^∞)}^{G(A^∞)} ⊕_{i≥0, w∈W^{R,p}} I^R(H̃^{s−i−ℓ(w)}(X(G_{h,R}), 𝒱_{λ(h,w)}) ⊗ H^i(X(G_{ℓ,R}), Ṽ_{λ(ℓ,w)})).
Here:
- for R = Π (Klingen), G_{h,R} = GSp2 = GL2 and G_{ℓ,R} = GL1;
- for R the Siegel parabolic or the Borel, G_{h,R} is trivial and G_{ℓ,R} = L_R (GL2 × GL1 or GL1^3);
- 𝒱_{λ(h,w)} is the canonical extension of an automorphic vector bundle on X(G_h);
- Ṽ_{λ(ℓ,w)} is the local system on X(G_ℓ) attached to an algebraic representation of G_ℓ, with highest weights as in [39, (3.6.1)];
- I_R is the intermediate induction of [40, (3.2.8)].

Hypotheses (as printed):
- Δ is a quotient of Δ_Q of p-power order;
- μ = (a,2) with p − 2 > a (the §7.2 standing hypothesis is p > a − 2);
- m is a non-Eisenstein maximal ideal of T_μ(Q);
- Assumption 7.7 holds for X_Δ(Q).
The proof actually requires H^2(X_0(Q), ω(a,2)(−∞)_k)_m = 0, i.e. Assumption 7.7 for Δ trivial [sourceIssues E79].
Conclusion: the O[Δ]-module M = H_0(X_Δ(Q), ω(1,3−a))_m = H^0(X_Δ(Q), ω(a,2)(−∞)_{K/O})^∨_m is balanced in the sense of Definition 3.2, i.e. d_{O[Δ]}(M) ≥ 0.

In the situation of item arthur-classes-b-f-excluded, suppose π̃ is of class (a). Then:
- π̃_∞ is essentially tempered (Clozel's purity lemma);
- hence π_∞ is essentially tempered, since its L-parameter is essentially bounded;
- by Theorem 5.6(3), π_∞ ≅ π(λ, C_i), the limit of discrete series with Harish-Chandra parameter λ = (a−1, 0; 4−a) and Weyl chamber C_i, where i is the cohomological degree;
- by Wallach's theorem, π is cuspidal.

Every π in the discrete automorphic spectrum A_(2)(G) of G = GSp4 over Q has a global Arthur parameter ψ. Its transfer π̃ to GL4(A) falls into one of six classes (a)–(f) [3, §5]:
- (a) general type: π̃ cuspidal on GL4 and χ_π-self-dual, ψ = π̃ ⊠ 1;
- (b) Yoshida type: μ_1 ⊞ μ_2;
- (c) Soudry type: μ|·|^{1/2} ⊞ μ|·|^{−1/2};
- (d) Saito–Kurokawa type: λ|·|^{1/2} ⊞ λ|·|^{−1/2} ⊞ μ;
- (e), (f): isobaric sums of idèle class characters.
Parts used:
- (ii) for ψ of type (a), S_ψ is trivial, so every π^∞ ⊗ π'_∞ with π'_∞ in the local packet Π_{ψ_∞} is automorphic (discrete);
- (iii) in case (a), discrete multiplicities are 1.
